**Proof.**

Yes, the closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{Q}_p$ with respect to the $p$-adic topology equals $\mathbb{Z}_p$.

Recall the relevant definitions and facts:

1. $\mathbb{Q}_p$ is the completion of $\mathbb{Q}$ with respect to the $p$-adic absolute value $|\cdot|_p$, and $\mathbb{Q}$ embeds densely into $\mathbb{Q}_p$.

2. $\mathbb{Z}_p = \{x \in \mathbb{Q}_p : |x|_p \le 1\}$ is the closed unit ball in $\mathbb{Q}_p$. Equivalently, $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is the completion of $\mathbb{Z}$ with respect to the $p$-adic metric (the restriction of $|\cdot|_p$ to $\mathbb{Z}$).

We prove $\overline{\mathbb{Z}} = \mathbb{Z}_p$ by showing both inclusions.

**($\overline{\mathbb{Z}} \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$):** Since $|n|_p \le 1$ for every $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, we have $\mathbb{Z} \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$. The set $\mathbb{Z}_p = \{x : |x|_p \le 1\}$ is closed in $\mathbb{Q}_p$ (it is the preimage of $[0,1]$ under the continuous map $x \mapsto |x|_p$). Therefore $\overline{\mathbb{Z}} \subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p$.

**($\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Z}}$):** It suffices to show $\mathbb{Z}$ is dense in $\mathbb{Z}_p$. Let $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ and $\varepsilon > 0$. Since $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense in $\mathbb{Q}_p$, there exists $q \in \mathbb{Q}$ with $|x - q|_p < \varepsilon$. We need to find an integer close to $x$. 

Write $q = a/b$ with $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$, $b \neq 0$. Choose $n \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $n \equiv a \pmod{b}$ (i.e., $b \mid (a - n)$, which is possible since we can take $n$ to be the remainder of $a$ upon division by $b$). Then $q - n = (a - n)/b \in \mathbb{Z}$, so $q - n \in \mathbb{Z}$, and $|q - n|_p \le 1$ (since every integer has $p$-adic absolute value $\le 1$). 

Now $x - n = (x - q) + (q - n)$, so $|x - n|_p \le \max(|x - q|_p, |q - n|_p)$. If $\varepsilon \le 1$, then $|x - q|_p < \varepsilon \le 1$ and $|q - n|_p \le 1$, giving $|x - n|_p \le 1$. But we need $|x - n|_p < \varepsilon$, not just $\le 1$.

Let me refine: choose $q$ with $|x - q|_p < \varepsilon$ and additionally $|q|_p \le 1$ (possible since $|x|_p \le 1$ and $\mathbb{Q} \cap \mathbb{Z}_p$ is dense in $\mathbb{Z}_p$, as $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is the closure of $\mathbb{Z}$... this is circular). 

Let me use a direct approach instead. Every $x \in \mathbb{Z}_p$ has a $p$-adic expansion $x = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k p^k$ with $a_k \in \{0, 1, \dots, p-1\}$. The partial sums $s_N = \sum_{k=0}^{N} a_k p^k$ are integers, and $|x - s_N|_p = |p^{N+1}|_p \cdot |\sum_{k=N+1}^{\infty} a_k p^{k-N-1}|_p \le p^{-(N+1)} \to 0$ as $N \to \infty$. Thus $s_N \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $s_N \to x$ in the $p$-adic topology, so $x \in \overline{\mathbb{Z}}$.

Hence $\mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Z}}$, and combined with the first inclusion, $\overline{\mathbb{Z}} = \mathbb{Z}_p$. $\blacksquare$

